8364 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419236 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198364 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419237 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198365 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419227 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198365 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419229 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198365 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419233 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198365 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419235 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198365 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419239 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419228 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419231 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419238 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419240 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419224 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419226 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419234 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419241 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198495 1050 1140.0 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198652 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ is enclosed between the curve $x={y}^{2}$ and $y$-axis for $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198652 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $λ(x, y) = xy^{2}$ is enclosed between the curve $x = y^{2}$ and $y$-axis for $-1 \\le y \\le 1$. Find the moment of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139534330 17592186045417 false] [17592186198652 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34c6fb2eead75cb5217e7b33b5b98400b3f82e8ec29c91de9b8db5b22cefa6d7.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{7}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{8}}{16}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198973 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=4y$ is enclosed between the line $x=y$ and the $y$-axis for $1\\le y\\le 2.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186198973 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $λ(x, y) = 4y$ is enclosed between the line $x = y$ and the $y$-axis for $1 \\le y \\le 2$. Find the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139534330 17592186045417 false] [17592186198973 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f8454539cbe01d45842ab03386e06bbbdca35d400d0e214563843d99cddd5a85.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=4y.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 4y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2x\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y({y}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186199455 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin lamina whose mass is unevenly distributed. \n\nWe describe the mass distribution across a lamina using its mass density function $\\lambda (x,y).$ This function tells us the *mass per unit area* at any point $(x,y)$ on the lamina.\n\nFor example, consider a thin plate with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = 6xy$ occupying the finite rectangular region $D$ between the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ What is the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $(x,y) \\in D$ on the plate and a small rectangular piece with its bottom-left corner at $(x,y)$ and area $\\Delta{A},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8cd947e542aaf7eb787abc7a846e87a2d03ccafa0987f3422928fdcba115da9e.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{A}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the moment of this piece about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{y} & \\approx x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & \\approx x\\cdot 6xy\\,ΔA \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y\\,ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total moment of the plate about the axis, we add together the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta{A} \\to 0.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nM_y =  \\iint\\limits_D 6x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(8-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe moment about the $x$-axis can be found similarly.\n\nIn general, the moments $M_x$ and $M_y$ of a lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis and about the $y$-axis, respectively, are given by the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534330 17592186045417 false] [17592186199455 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin lamina whose mass is unevenly distributed. \n\nWe describe the mass distribution across a lamina using its mass density function $\\lambda (x,y).$ This function tells us the *mass per unit area* at any point $(x,y)$ on the lamina.\n\nFor example, consider a thin plate with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = 6xy$ occupying the finite rectangular region $D$ between the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ What is the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $(x,y) \\in D$ on the plate and a small rectangular piece with its bottom-left corner at $(x,y)$ and area $\\Delta{A},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8cd947e542aaf7eb787abc7a846e87a2d03ccafa0987f3422928fdcba115da9e.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{A}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the moment of this piece about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{y} & ≈x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & ≈x\\cdot 6xy\\,ΔA \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y\\,ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total moment of the plate about the axis, we add together the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta{A} \\to 0.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nM_y =  \\iint\\limits_D 6x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(8-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe moment about the $x$-axis can be found similarly.\n\nIn general, the moments $M_x$ and $M_y$ of a lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis and about the $y$-axis, respectively, are given by the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419116 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419116 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419116 1083 #uuid "01cd82d5-f5f6-5657-8791-e77a91fb2d8f" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419117 1080 "2" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419117 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419117 1083 #uuid "020556d4-2945-5f3c-aaf3-c713eaecc1c5" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419118 1080 "\\frac{e+1}{4}" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419118 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419118 1083 #uuid "020ac850-b2cd-5d3f-bd80-a39b02f4b24f" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419119 1080 "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419119 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534330 17592186045417 t